> Thank you for your thoughtful email and for sharing your background. Your work on water-resource policy, environmental governance, and sustainable development in Bangladesh aligns well with several areas of my research.
> 
> I am not currently taking on new PhD students, either in Environmental Economics or Marine Policy, so I would not be able to supervise a PhD application.
> 
> If you are considering a master’s degree, you may wish to explore the Environmental Economics (EE) Master’s program or the Environmental Science & Management (ESM) program. The former Master’s in Marine Policy program has transitioned into the new ESM program, which includes a Marine track:  
> https://www.udel.edu/academics/colleges/ceoe/prospective-students/graduate/environmental-science-and-management/
> 
> Please note that both the EE Master’s and ESM programs are self-funded.
> 
> For doctoral study, the Environmental Economics PhD is generally the best fit for students seeking rigorous, quantitatively oriented research training in environmental and climate policy. The Marine Policy PhD may also be worth considering, particularly for students with a less quantitative or more nontraditional background, though I am not accepting students in either program. If your interests are instead primarily in natural-science marine research, I suggest contacting Professor Carlos Moffat at cmoffat@udel.edu.
> 
> I wish you the best in identifying the right program and supervisor for your research goals.
